WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:03.660
- [Instructor] I suspect that
you might already be familiar

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.090
with the term mixture.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.310
It really does mean
what you think it means.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:09.980
If you take two or more substances

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.830
and you were to mix them together,

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:13.870
you are dealing with a mixture.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.610
And it could be a solid,
a liquid or a gas.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.800
Now there's fundamentally two
different types of mixtures.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.360
There's heterogeneous mixtures.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.020
And hetero, you're going
to see that prefix a lot,

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.540
means different.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:32.540
So heterogeneous, this
is referring to mixtures

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.090
that if you look at it
from a macro point of view,

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.950
I'll do a heterogeneous liquid mixture.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.520
And what I often think of
here at the extreme form

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:42.660
is where with my naked eye,

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.130
I could see that different
parts of the mixtures

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.960
have different concentrations
of the different things

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.210
that have been mixed up.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.580
One example I often think
of is chocolate milk

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.300
that's not well mixed.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.210
At the top you're going
to see these clumps

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.120
and depending where you are in the milk,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.598
you can actually see it with your eye,

00:00:59.598 --> 00:01:02.190
that you don't have
consistent concentrations

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.540
of the different substances.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.430
The properties of the
mixtures are different,

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:10.240
depending on what part of
the mixture you look at.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.580
Now, you can imagine if
there's heterogeneous mixtures,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.140
the opposite would be
homogeneous mixtures.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.320
Those would be mixtures
that when you look at it

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.230
from a macro point of
view, it looks consistent

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.450
any point of the mixture.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.820
Homogeneous mixtures.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:32.500
And homogeneous mixtures,
there's another term for it,

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.170
which you have probably heard.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:37.193
They're also known as solutions.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.460
And as I mentioned, when
we talk about mixtures,

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:42.740
we could be talking about
solid, liquid or gas.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.810
Oftentimes in our head, we
think liquid immediately,

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.360
but it can be solid liquid or gas.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.340
And so the same thing
is true for solutions.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.040
You can have solid liquids or gases.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.970
And an example of a
solid solution would be,

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.960
say a metal alloy.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:02.010
Where at the macro level it's color,

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.570
how well does it conduct electricity?

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.400
How malleable is it?

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:07.840
How hard is it?

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.920
It looks like the properties are uniform

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.050
throughout the alloy.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.220
If I have some type of metal alloy,

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:17.610
if I were to just look at it like this,

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.240
if I were to look at say bronze,

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.010
well, from my point of view,

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.920
it has the same properties
throughout the bronze.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.610
And I should probably do
bronze in a more bronze color

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:28.660
right over here.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:29.970
But if you were to look at it

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.030
with a very sensitive microscope

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:33.160
or based on the models

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:36.100
that we now know what is
going on in that metal,

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.340
in that alloy,

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:42.340
what's happening is you actually
have a mixture of metals.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:46.840
But it is a homogeneous mixture of metals

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.070
because at a macro level,

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:51.410
you can't really see the differences.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:53.280
Now at a micro level, you can,

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.200
because there are different
metals mixed in together

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.180
in this alloy.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:03.180
Now, as can imagine you
also have liquid solutions

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.570
and in chemistry,

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:06.640
these are the ones that
we'll often deal most.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.860
We will also deal with solid
and gas solutions as well.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.440
And when I think of a
homogeneous liquid mixture

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.040
or a liquid solution, the one
that comes to mind for me,

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:17.290
but we'll see a lot of these

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:20.573
in our journey through
chemistry is salt water.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:23.410
Salt water.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:28.110
Salt water has sodium
chloride dissolved in water.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:29.850
And if you were to go to the ocean,

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:31.940
and if you were to just
look at the salt water,

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.550
or if you were to measure its
properties, even in a lab,

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:37.560
you would see that the properties
throughout the salt water

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.160
seem to be uniform.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:42.880
At a macro level, it
appears to be homogeneous.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.660
It appears to be uniform.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.730
But if you were to look
at what's happening

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.160
at an atomic level,

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.080
you would see that you
have your water molecules.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.340
Let me draw the water like that.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:58.600
That is the oxygen, each
of them has two hydrogens.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.330
And this end is partially positive,

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:05.520
we've seen this multiple
times, the hydrogen end,

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.300
the oxygen end is partially negative.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.610
And so when you dissolve
the sodium chloride,

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:13.780
the chloride anions

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.770
are attracted to the
positive ends of the water.

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:20.070
So maybe you have a chloride anion there.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.660
And the sodium ions

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.220
would be attracted to the
negative end of the H2O.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.250
So that's the sodium right over there.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.370
So when you look at it
at a micro perspective,

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.290
you see that there are
different molecules,

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:36.300
but at a macro perspective,
the macro properties,

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.560
how it responds to different stimulus,

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.010
how well it conducts electricity,
what it even looks like,

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:44.340
it seems to be uniform.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:48.050
Now to be clear, saltwater is
not the only liquid solution.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:49.890
There's many types of liquid solutions.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.830
In chemistry, we'll often see water

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:54.430
as our most common solvent.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.570
That's the thing that
you have the most of,

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.200
the thing that other things
are getting dissolved in it.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.460
The things that are getting dissolved

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:02.340
are called the solute,

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:05.530
in this case it would
be the sodium chloride

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:08.200
and the water is the solvent.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:09.883
Let me write that down, solvent.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:12.710
When you have a solution

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.040
where you have something
dissolved in water,

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.973
this is often called an
aqueous, aqueous solution.

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:23.413
And sometimes it's abbreviated aq.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:28.700
Now, last but not least, we
haven't talked about gases yet.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:32.930
And you can for sure
have a solution of gas.

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:34.330
And the one that's most common

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:36.830
is the one that you and I
are breathing right now.

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:40.113
The air around you is a gas solution.

00:05:41.619 --> 00:05:44.380
It's macroscopic
properties seem consistent,

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:45.770
they seem uniform,

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:50.190
but we know that the gas around
us is a mixture of nitrogen,

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:52.710
of oxygen, of carbon dioxide,

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.840
as well as molecules of other things.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:00.840
So a good example of a
gas solution would be air.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.570
So I'll let you go there.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:05.650
That's just a good primer
on what a mixture is,

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:06.750
the types of mixtures

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.700
and especially the subtype of a mixture,

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:11.260
which is a homogeneous mixture,

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.693
which we often call a solution.

